#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-rebase(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:7
msgid "git-rebase - Reapply commits on top of another base tip"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"'git rebase' [-i | --interactive] [<options>] [--exec <cmd>]\n"
"\t[--onto <newbase> | --keep-base] [<upstream> [<branch>]]\n"
"'git rebase' [-i | --interactive] [<options>] [--exec <cmd>] [--onto "
"<newbase>]\n"
"\t--root [<branch>]\n"
"'git rebase' "
"(--continue|--skip|--abort|--quit|--edit-todo|--show-current-patch)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:22
msgid ""
"Transplant a series of commits onto a different starting point.  You can "
"also use `git rebase` to reorder or combine commits: see INTERACTIVE MODE "
"below for how to do that."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:25
msgid ""
"For example, imagine that you have been working on the `topic` branch in "
"this history, and you want to \"catch up\" to the work done on the `master` "
"branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:30
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          A---B---C topic\n"
"         /\n"
"    D---E---F---G master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:37
msgid ""
"You want to transplant the commits you made on `topic` since it diverged "
"from `master` (i.e. A, B, and C), on top of the current `master`.  You can "
"do this by running `git rebase master` while the `topic` branch is checked "
"out.  If you want to rebase `topic` while on another branch, `git rebase "
"master topic` is a shortcut for `git checkout topic && git rebase master`."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                  A'--B'--C' topic\n"
"                 /\n"
"    D---E---F---G master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:48
msgid ""
"If there is a merge conflict during this process, `git rebase` will stop at "
"the first problematic commit and leave conflict markers. If this happens, "
"you can do one of these things:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:54
msgid ""
"Resolve the conflict. You can use `git diff` to find the markers (<<<<<<)  "
"and make edits to resolve the conflict. For each file you edit, you need to "
"tell Git that the conflict has been resolved. You can mark the conflict as "
"resolved with `git add <filename>`. After resolving all of the conflicts, "
"you can continue the rebasing process with"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "git rebase --continue\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:58
msgid "Stop the `git rebase` and return your branch to its original state with"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "git rebase --abort\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:62
msgid "Skip the commit that caused the merge conflict with"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:64
#, no-wrap
msgid "git rebase --skip\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:70
msgid ""
"If you don't specify an `<upstream>` to rebase onto, the upstream configured "
"in `branch.<name>.remote` and `branch.<name>.merge` options will be used "
"(see linkgit:git-config[1] for details) and the `--fork-point` option is "
"assumed.  If you are currently not on any branch or if the current branch "
"does not have a configured upstream, the rebase will abort."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:72
msgid "Here is a simplified description of what `git rebase <upstream>` does:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:76
msgid ""
"Make a list of all commits on your current branch since it branched off from "
"`<upstream>` that do not have an equivalent commit in `<upstream>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:78
msgid ""
"Check out `<upstream>` with the equivalent of `git checkout --detach "
"<upstream>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:81
msgid ""
"Replay the commits, one by one, in order. This is similar to running `git "
"cherry-pick <commit>` for each commit. See REBASING MERGES for how merges "
"are handled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:83
msgid ""
"Update your branch to point to the final commit with the equivalent of `git "
"checkout -B <branch>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:91
msgid ""
"When starting the rebase, `ORIG_HEAD` is set to point to the commit at the "
"tip of the to-be-rebased branch. However, `ORIG_HEAD` is not guaranteed to "
"still point to that commit at the end of the rebase if other commands that "
"change `ORIG_HEAD` (like `git reset`) are used during the rebase. The "
"previous branch tip, however, is accessible using the reflog of the current "
"branch (i.e. `@{1}`, see linkgit:gitrevisions[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:93
#, no-wrap
msgid "TRANSPLANTING A TOPIC BRANCH WITH --ONTO"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:98
msgid ""
"Here is how you would transplant a topic branch based on one branch to "
"another, to pretend that you forked the topic branch from the latter branch, "
"using `rebase --onto`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:102
msgid ""
"First let's assume your 'topic' is based on branch 'next'.  For example, a "
"feature developed in 'topic' depends on some functionality which is found in "
"'next'."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    o---o---o---o---o  master\n"
"         \\\n"
"          o---o---o---o---o  next\n"
"                           \\\n"
"                            o---o---o  topic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:114
msgid ""
"We want to make 'topic' forked from branch 'master'; for example, because "
"the functionality on which 'topic' depends was merged into the more stable "
"'master' branch. We want our tree to look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:121
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    o---o---o---o---o  master\n"
"        |            \\\n"
"        |             o'--o'--o'  topic\n"
"         \\\n"
"          o---o---o---o---o  next\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:124
msgid "We can get this using the following command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:126
#, no-wrap
msgid "    git rebase --onto master next topic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:130
msgid ""
"Another example of --onto option is to rebase part of a branch.  If we have "
"the following situation:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                            H---I---J topicB\n"
"                           /\n"
"                  E---F---G  topicA\n"
"                 /\n"
"    A---B---C---D  master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:140
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:163
msgid "then the command"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "    git rebase --onto master topicA topicB\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:144
msgid "would result in:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:151
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                 H'--I'--J'  topicB\n"
"                /\n"
"                | E---F---G  topicA\n"
"                |/\n"
"    A---B---C---D  master\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:154
msgid "This is useful when topicB does not depend on topicA."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:157
msgid ""
"A range of commits could also be removed with rebase.  If we have the "
"following situation:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "    E---F---G---H---I---J  topicA\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:165
#, no-wrap
msgid "    git rebase --onto topicA~5 topicA~3 topicA\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:167
msgid "would result in the removal of commits F and G:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "    E---H'---I'---J'  topicA\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:175
msgid ""
"This is useful if F and G were flawed in some way, or should not be part of "
"topicA.  Note that the argument to `--onto` and the `<upstream>` parameter "
"can be any valid commit-ish."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "MODE OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:181
msgid ""
"The options in this section cannot be used with any other option, including "
"not with each other:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:182
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--continue`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:184
msgid "Restart the rebasing process after having resolved a merge conflict."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--skip`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:187
msgid "Restart the rebasing process by skipping the current patch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:188
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--abort`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:194
msgid ""
"Abort the rebase operation and reset HEAD to the original branch. If "
"`<branch>` was provided when the rebase operation was started, then `HEAD` "
"will be reset to `<branch>`. Otherwise `HEAD` will be reset to where it was "
"when the rebase operation was started."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quit`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:200
msgid ""
"Abort the rebase operation but `HEAD` is not reset back to the original "
"branch. The index and working tree are also left unchanged as a result. If a "
"temporary stash entry was created using `--autostash`, it will be saved to "
"the stash list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:201
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--edit-todo`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:203
msgid "Edit the todo list during an interactive rebase."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:204
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--show-current-patch`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:208
msgid ""
"Show the current patch in an interactive rebase or when rebase is stopped "
"because of conflicts. This is the equivalent of `git show REBASE_HEAD`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:210
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:211
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--onto <newbase>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:216
msgid ""
"Starting point at which to create the new commits. If the `--onto` option is "
"not specified, the starting point is `<upstream>`.  May be any valid commit, "
"and not just an existing branch name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:220
msgid ""
"As a special case, you may use \"A\\...B\" as a shortcut for the merge base "
"of A and B if there is exactly one merge base. You can leave out at most one "
"of A and B, in which case it defaults to HEAD."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:222
msgid "See TRANSPLANTING A TOPIC BRANCH WITH --ONTO above for examples."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:223
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--keep-base`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:229
msgid ""
"Set the starting point at which to create the new commits to the merge base "
"of `<upstream>` and `<branch>`. Running `git rebase --keep-base <upstream> "
"<branch>` is equivalent to running `git rebase --reapply-cherry-picks "
"--no-fork-point --onto <upstream>...<branch> <upstream> <branch>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:236
msgid ""
"This option is useful in the case where one is developing a feature on top "
"of an upstream branch. While the feature is being worked on, the upstream "
"branch may advance and it may not be the best idea to keep rebasing on top "
"of the upstream but to keep the base commit as-is. As the base commit is "
"unchanged this option implies `--reapply-cherry-picks` to avoid losing "
"commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:241
msgid ""
"Although both this option and `--fork-point` find the merge base between "
"`<upstream>` and `<branch>`, this option uses the merge base as the "
"_starting point_ on which new commits will be created, whereas "
"`--fork-point` uses the merge base to determine the _set of commits_ which "
"will be rebased."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:243
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:258
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:286
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:307
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:329
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:337
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:349
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:361
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:370
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:412
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:447
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:472
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:485
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:492
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:504
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:557
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:564
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:590
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:625
msgid "See also INCOMPATIBLE OPTIONS below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:244
#, no-wrap
msgid "<upstream>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:248
msgid ""
"Upstream branch to compare against.  May be any valid commit, not just an "
"existing branch name. Defaults to the configured upstream for the current "
"branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:249
#, no-wrap
msgid "<branch>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:251
msgid "Working branch; defaults to `HEAD`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:252
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:632
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--apply`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:256
msgid ""
"Use applying strategies to rebase (calling `git-am` internally).  This "
"option may become a no-op in the future once the merge backend handles "
"everything the apply one does."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:259
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--empty=(drop|keep|stop)`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:264
msgid ""
"How to handle commits that are not empty to start and are not clean "
"cherry-picks of any upstream commit, but which become empty after rebasing "
"(because they contain a subset of already upstream changes):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:266
#, no-wrap
msgid "`drop`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:268
msgid "The commit will be dropped. This is the default behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:268
#, no-wrap
msgid "`keep`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:271
msgid ""
"The commit will be kept. This option is implied when `--exec` is specified "
"unless `-i`/`--interactive` is also specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:271
#, no-wrap
msgid "`stop`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:272
#, no-wrap
msgid "`ask`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:277
msgid ""
"The rebase will halt when the commit is applied, allowing you to choose "
"whether to drop it, edit files more, or just commit the empty changes. This "
"option is implied when `-i`/`--interactive` is specified. `ask` is a "
"deprecated synonym of `stop`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:284
msgid ""
"Note that commits which start empty are kept (unless `--no-keep-empty` is "
"specified), and commits which are clean cherry-picks (as determined by `git "
"log --cherry-mark ...`) are detected and dropped as a preliminary step "
"(unless `--reapply-cherry-picks` or `--keep-base` is passed)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:287
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:645
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-keep-empty`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:288
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--keep-empty`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:296
msgid ""
"Do not keep commits that start empty before the rebase (i.e. that do not "
"change anything from its parent) in the result.  The default is to keep "
"commits which start empty, since creating such commits requires passing the "
"`--allow-empty` override flag to `git commit`, signifying that a user is "
"very intentionally creating such a commit and thus wants to keep it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:302
msgid ""
"Usage of this flag will probably be rare, since you can get rid of commits "
"that start empty by just firing up an interactive rebase and removing the "
"lines corresponding to the commits you don't want.  This flag exists as a "
"convenient shortcut, such as for cases where external tools generate many "
"empty commits and you want them all removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:305
msgid ""
"For commits which do not start empty but become empty after rebasing, see "
"the `--empty` flag."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:308
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reapply-cherry-picks`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:309
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-reapply-cherry-picks`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:315
msgid ""
"Reapply all clean cherry-picks of any upstream commit instead of "
"preemptively dropping them. (If these commits then become empty after "
"rebasing, because they contain a subset of already upstream changes, the "
"behavior towards them is controlled by the `--empty` flag.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:324
msgid ""
"In the absence of `--keep-base` (or if `--no-reapply-cherry-picks` is "
"given), these commits will be automatically dropped.  Because this "
"necessitates reading all upstream commits, this can be expensive in "
"repositories with a large number of upstream commits that need to be "
"read. When using the 'merge' backend, warnings will be issued for each "
"dropped commit (unless `--quiet` is given). Advice will also be issued "
"unless `advice.skippedCherryPicks` is set to false (see "
"linkgit:git-config[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:327
msgid ""
"`--reapply-cherry-picks` allows rebase to forgo reading all upstream "
"commits, potentially improving performance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:330
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--allow-empty-message`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:335
msgid ""
"No-op.  Rebasing commits with an empty message used to fail and this option "
"would override that behavior, allowing commits with empty messages to be "
"rebased.  Now commits with an empty message do not cause rebasing to halt."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:338
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-m`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:339
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:638
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--merge`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:341
msgid "Using merging strategies to rebase (default)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:347
msgid ""
"Note that a rebase merge works by replaying each commit from the working "
"branch on top of the `<upstream>` branch.  Because of this, when a merge "
"conflict happens, the side reported as 'ours' is the so-far rebased series, "
"starting with `<upstream>`, and 'theirs' is the working branch.  In other "
"words, the sides are swapped."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:350
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-s <strategy>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:351
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--strategy=<strategy>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:354
msgid ""
"Use the given merge strategy, instead of the default `ort`.  This implies "
"`--merge`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:359
msgid ""
"Because `git rebase` replays each commit from the working branch on top of "
"the `<upstream>` branch using the given strategy, using the `ours` strategy "
"simply empties all patches from the `<branch>`, which makes little sense."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:362
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-X <strategy-option>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:363
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--strategy-option=<strategy-option>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:368
msgid ""
"Pass the <strategy-option> through to the merge strategy.  This implies "
"`--merge` and, if no strategy has been specified, `-s ort`.  Note the "
"reversal of 'ours' and 'theirs' as noted above for the `-m` option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:373
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-S[<keyid>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--gpg-sign[=<keyid>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:375
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-gpg-sign`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:381
msgid ""
"GPG-sign commits. The `keyid` argument is optional and defaults to the "
"committer identity; if specified, it must be stuck to the option without a "
"space. `--no-gpg-sign` is useful to countermand both `commit.gpgSign` "
"configuration variable, and earlier `--gpg-sign`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:382
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:383
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:385
msgid "Be quiet. Implies `--no-stat`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:386
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-v`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:387
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verbose`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:389
msgid "Be verbose. Implies `--stat`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:390
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--stat`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:393
msgid ""
"Show a diffstat of what changed upstream since the last rebase. The diffstat "
"is also controlled by the configuration option rebase.stat."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:394
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:395
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-stat`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:397
msgid "Do not show a diffstat as part of the rebase process."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:398
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-verify`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:400
msgid "This option bypasses the pre-rebase hook.  See also linkgit:githooks[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:401
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verify`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:404
msgid ""
"Allows the pre-rebase hook to run, which is the default.  This option can be "
"used to override `--no-verify`.  See also linkgit:githooks[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:405
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-C<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:410
msgid ""
"Ensure at least `<n>` lines of surrounding context match before and after "
"each change.  When fewer lines of surrounding context exist they all must "
"match.  By default no context is ever ignored.  Implies `--apply`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:413
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-ff`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:414
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force-rebase`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:415
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-f`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:419
msgid ""
"Individually replay all rebased commits instead of fast-forwarding over the "
"unchanged ones.  This ensures that the entire history of the rebased branch "
"is composed of new commits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:425
msgid ""
"You may find this helpful after reverting a topic branch merge, as this "
"option recreates the topic branch with fresh commits so it can be remerged "
"successfully without needing to \"revert the reversion\" (see the "
"link:howto/revert-a-faulty-merge.html[revert-a-faulty-merge How-To] for "
"details)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:426
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--fork-point`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:427
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-fork-point`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:431
msgid ""
"Use reflog to find a better common ancestor between `<upstream>` and "
"`<branch>` when calculating which commits have been introduced by "
"`<branch>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:437
msgid ""
"When `--fork-point` is active, 'fork_point' will be used instead of "
"`<upstream>` to calculate the set of commits to rebase, where 'fork_point' "
"is the result of `git merge-base --fork-point <upstream> <branch>` command "
"(see linkgit:git-merge-base[1]).  If 'fork_point' ends up being empty, the "
"`<upstream>` will be used as a fallback."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:441
msgid ""
"If `<upstream>` or `--keep-base` is given on the command line, then the "
"default is `--no-fork-point`, otherwise the default is `--fork-point`. See "
"also `rebase.forkpoint` in linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:445
msgid ""
"If your branch was based on `<upstream>` but `<upstream>` was rewound and "
"your branch contains commits which were dropped, this option can be used "
"with `--keep-base` in order to drop those commits from your branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:448
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ignore-whitespace`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:452
msgid ""
"Ignore whitespace differences when trying to reconcile "
"differences. Currently, each backend implements an approximation of this "
"behavior:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:453
#, no-wrap
msgid "apply backend"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:459
msgid ""
"When applying a patch, ignore changes in whitespace in context "
"lines. Unfortunately, this means that if the \"old\" lines being replaced by "
"the patch differ only in whitespace from the existing file, you will get a "
"merge conflict instead of a successful patch application."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:460
#, no-wrap
msgid "merge backend"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:465
msgid ""
"Treat lines with only whitespace changes as unchanged when merging.  "
"Unfortunately, this means that any patch hunks that were intended to modify "
"whitespace and nothing else will be dropped, even if the other side had no "
"changes that conflicted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:466
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--whitespace=<option>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:470
msgid ""
"This flag is passed to the `git apply` program (see linkgit:git-apply[1]) "
"that applies the patch.  Implies `--apply`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:473
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--committer-date-is-author-date`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:477
msgid ""
"Instead of using the current time as the committer date, use the author date "
"of the commit being rebased as the committer date. This option implies "
"`--force-rebase`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:478
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ignore-date`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:479
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reset-author-date`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:483
msgid ""
"Instead of using the author date of the original commit, use the current "
"time as the\tauthor date of the rebased commit.  This option implies "
"`--force-rebase`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:486
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--signoff`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:490
msgid ""
"Add a `Signed-off-by` trailer to all the rebased commits. Note that if "
"`--interactive` is given then only commits marked to be picked, edited or "
"reworded will have the trailer added."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:493
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-i`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:494
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:643
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--interactive`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:498
msgid ""
"Make a list of the commits which are about to be rebased.  Let the user edit "
"that list before rebasing.  This mode can also be used to split commits (see "
"SPLITTING COMMITS below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:502
msgid ""
"The commit list format can be changed by setting the configuration option "
"rebase.instructionFormat.  A customized instruction format will "
"automatically have the commit hash prepended to the format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:505
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-r`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:506
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--rebase-merges[=(rebase-cousins|no-rebase-cousins)]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:507
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-rebase-merges`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:517
msgid ""
"By default, a rebase will simply drop merge commits from the todo list, and "
"put the rebased commits into a single, linear branch.  With "
"`--rebase-merges`, the rebase will instead try to preserve the branching "
"structure within the commits that are to be rebased, by recreating the merge "
"commits. Any resolved merge conflicts or manual amendments in these merge "
"commits will have to be resolved/re-applied manually. `--no-rebase-merges` "
"can be used to countermand both the `rebase.rebaseMerges` config option and "
"a previous `--rebase-merges`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:526
msgid ""
"When rebasing merges, there are two modes: `rebase-cousins` and "
"`no-rebase-cousins`. If the mode is not specified, it defaults to "
"`no-rebase-cousins`. In `no-rebase-cousins` mode, commits which do not have "
"`<upstream>` as direct ancestor will keep their original branch point, i.e.  "
"commits that would be excluded by linkgit:git-log[1]'s `--ancestry-path` "
"option will keep their original ancestry by default. In `rebase-cousins` "
"mode, such commits are instead rebased onto `<upstream>` (or `<onto>`, if "
"specified)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:530
msgid ""
"It is currently only possible to recreate the merge commits using the `ort` "
"merge strategy; different merge strategies can be used only via explicit "
"`exec git merge -s <strategy> [...]` commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:532
msgid "See also REBASING MERGES and INCOMPATIBLE OPTIONS below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:533
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-x <cmd>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:534
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--exec <cmd>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:539
msgid ""
"Append \"exec <cmd>\" after each line creating a commit in the final "
"history. `<cmd>` will be interpreted as one or more shell commands. Any "
"command that fails will interrupt the rebase, with exit code 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:542
msgid ""
"You may execute several commands by either using one instance of `--exec` "
"with several commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:544
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tgit rebase -i --exec \"cmd1 && cmd2 && ...\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:546
msgid "or by giving more than one `--exec`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:548
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tgit rebase -i --exec \"cmd1\" --exec \"cmd2\" --exec ...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:552
msgid ""
"If `--autosquash` is used, `exec` lines will not be appended for the "
"intermediate commits, and will only appear at the end of each squash/fixup "
"series."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:555
msgid ""
"This uses the `--interactive` machinery internally, but it can be run "
"without an explicit `--interactive`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:558
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--root`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:562
msgid ""
"Rebase all commits reachable from `<branch>`, instead of limiting them with "
"an `<upstream>`.  This allows you to rebase the root commit(s) on a branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:565
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:641
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--autosquash`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:566
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-autosquash`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:574
msgid ""
"Automatically squash commits with specially formatted messages into previous "
"commits being rebased.  If a commit message starts with \"squash! \", "
"\"fixup! \" or \"amend! \", the remainder of the title is taken as a commit "
"specifier, which matches a previous commit if it matches the title or the "
"hash of that commit.  If no commit matches fully, matches of the specifier "
"with the start of commit titles are considered."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:579
msgid ""
"In the rebase todo list, the actions of squash, fixup and amend commits are "
"changed from `pick` to `squash`, `fixup` or `fixup -C`, respectively, and "
"they are moved right after the commit they modify.  The `--interactive` "
"option can be used to review and edit the todo list before proceeding."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:584
msgid ""
"The recommended way to create commits with squash markers is by using the "
"`--squash`, `--fixup`, `--fixup=amend:` or `--fixup=reword:` options of "
"linkgit:git-commit[1], which take the target commit as an argument and "
"automatically fill in the title of the new commit from that."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:588
msgid ""
"Setting configuration variable `rebase.autoSquash` to true enables "
"auto-squashing by default for interactive rebase.  The `--no-autosquash` "
"option can be used to override that setting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:591
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--autostash`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:592
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-autostash`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:598
msgid ""
"Automatically create a temporary stash entry before the operation begins, "
"and apply it after the operation ends.  This means that you can run rebase "
"on a dirty worktree.  However, use with care: the final stash application "
"after a successful rebase might result in non-trivial conflicts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:599
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reschedule-failed-exec`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:600
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-reschedule-failed-exec`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:603
msgid ""
"Automatically reschedule `exec` commands that failed. This only makes sense "
"in interactive mode (or when an `--exec` option was provided)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:608
msgid ""
"This option applies once a rebase is started. It is preserved for the whole "
"rebase based on, in order, the command line option provided to the initial "
"`git rebase`, the `rebase.rescheduleFailedExec` configuration (see "
"linkgit:git-config[1] or \"CONFIGURATION\" below), or it defaults to false."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:614
msgid ""
"Recording this option for the whole rebase is a convenience "
"feature. Otherwise an explicit `--no-reschedule-failed-exec` at the start "
"would be overridden by the presence of a `rebase.rescheduleFailedExec=true` "
"configuration when `git rebase --continue` is invoked. Currently, you cannot "
"pass `--[no-]reschedule-failed-exec` to `git rebase --continue`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:615
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:648
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--update-refs`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:616
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-update-refs`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:620
msgid ""
"Automatically force-update any branches that point to commits that are being "
"rebased. Any branches that are checked out in a worktree are not updated in "
"this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:623
msgid ""
"If the configuration variable `rebase.updateRefs` is set, then this option "
"can be used to override and disable this setting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:627
#, no-wrap
msgid "INCOMPATIBLE OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:630
msgid "The following options:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:633
msgid "`--whitespace`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:634
msgid "`-C`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:636
msgid "are incompatible with the following options:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:639
msgid "`--strategy`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:640
msgid "`--strategy-option`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:642
msgid "`--rebase-merges`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:644
msgid "`--exec`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:646
msgid "`--empty=`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:647
msgid "`--[no-]reapply-cherry-picks` when used without `--keep-base`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:649
msgid "`--root` when used without `--onto`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:651
msgid "In addition, the following pairs of options are incompatible:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:653
msgid "`--keep-base` and `--onto`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:654
msgid "`--keep-base` and `--root`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:655
msgid "`--fork-point` and `--root`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:657
#, no-wrap
msgid "BEHAVIORAL DIFFERENCES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:666
msgid ""
"`git rebase` has two primary backends: 'apply' and 'merge'.  (The 'apply' "
"backend used to be known as the 'am' backend, but the name led to confusion "
"as it looks like a verb instead of a noun.  Also, the 'merge' backend used "
"to be known as the interactive backend, but it is now used for "
"non-interactive cases as well.  Both were renamed based on lower-level "
"functionality that underpinned each.) There are some subtle differences in "
"how these two backends behave:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:667
#, no-wrap
msgid "Empty commits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:674
msgid ""
"The 'apply' backend unfortunately drops intentionally empty commits, i.e.  "
"commits that started empty, though these are rare in practice.  It also "
"drops commits that become empty and has no option for controlling this "
"behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:678
msgid ""
"The 'merge' backend keeps intentionally empty commits by default (though "
"with `-i` they are marked as empty in the todo list editor, or they can be "
"dropped automatically with `--no-keep-empty`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:684
msgid ""
"Similar to the apply backend, by default the merge backend drops commits "
"that become empty unless `-i`/`--interactive` is specified (in which case it "
"stops and asks the user what to do).  The merge backend also has an "
"`--empty=(drop|keep|stop)` option for changing the behavior of handling "
"commits that become empty."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:685
#, no-wrap
msgid "Directory rename detection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:696
msgid ""
"Due to the lack of accurate tree information (arising from constructing fake "
"ancestors with the limited information available in patches), directory "
"rename detection is disabled in the 'apply' backend.  Disabled directory "
"rename detection means that if one side of history renames a directory and "
"the other adds new files to the old directory, then the new files will be "
"left behind in the old directory without any warning at the time of rebasing "
"that you may want to move these files into the new directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:699
msgid ""
"Directory rename detection works with the 'merge' backend to provide you "
"warnings in such cases."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:700
#, no-wrap
msgid "Context"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:717
msgid ""
"The 'apply' backend works by creating a sequence of patches (by calling "
"`format-patch` internally), and then applying the patches in sequence "
"(calling `am` internally).  Patches are composed of multiple hunks, each "
"with line numbers, a context region, and the actual changes.  The line "
"numbers have to be taken with some offset, since the other side will likely "
"have inserted or deleted lines earlier in the file.  The context region is "
"meant to help find how to adjust the line numbers in order to apply the "
"changes to the right lines.  However, if multiple areas of the code have the "
"same surrounding lines of context, the wrong one can be picked.  There are "
"real-world cases where this has caused commits to be reapplied incorrectly "
"with no conflicts reported.  Setting `diff.context` to a larger value may "
"prevent such types of problems, but increases the chance of spurious "
"conflicts (since it will require more lines of matching context to apply)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:720
msgid ""
"The 'merge' backend works with a full copy of each relevant file, insulating "
"it from these types of problems."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:721
#, no-wrap
msgid "Labelling of conflicts markers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:734
msgid ""
"When there are content conflicts, the merge machinery tries to annotate each "
"side's conflict markers with the commits where the content came from.  Since "
"the 'apply' backend drops the original information about the rebased commits "
"and their parents (and instead generates new fake commits based off limited "
"information in the generated patches), those commits cannot be identified; "
"instead it has to fall back to a commit summary.  Also, when "
"`merge.conflictStyle` is set to `diff3` or `zdiff3`, the 'apply' backend "
"will use \"constructed merge base\" to label the content from the merge "
"base, and thus provide no information about the merge base commit "
"whatsoever."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:737
msgid ""
"The 'merge' backend works with the full commits on both sides of history and "
"thus has no such limitations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:738
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hooks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:753
msgid ""
"The 'apply' backend has not traditionally called the post-commit hook, while "
"the 'merge' backend has.  Both have called the post-checkout hook, though "
"the 'merge' backend has squelched its output.  Further, both backends only "
"call the post-checkout hook with the starting point commit of the rebase, "
"not the intermediate commits nor the final commit.  In each case, the "
"calling of these hooks was by accident of implementation rather than by "
"design (both backends were originally implemented as shell scripts and "
"happened to invoke other commands like `git checkout` or `git commit` that "
"would call the hooks).  Both backends should have the same behavior, though "
"it is not entirely clear which, if any, is correct.  We will likely make "
"rebase stop calling either of these hooks in the future."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:754
#, no-wrap
msgid "Interruptability"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:764
msgid ""
"The 'apply' backend has safety problems with an ill-timed interrupt; if the "
"user presses Ctrl-C at the wrong time to try to abort the rebase, the rebase "
"can enter a state where it cannot be aborted with a subsequent `git rebase "
"--abort`.  The 'merge' backend does not appear to suffer from the same "
"shortcoming.  (See "
"https://lore.kernel.org/git/20200207132152.GC2868@szeder.dev/ for details.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:765
#, no-wrap
msgid "Commit Rewording"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:774
msgid ""
"When a conflict occurs while rebasing, rebase stops and asks the user to "
"resolve.  Since the user may need to make notable changes while resolving "
"conflicts, after conflicts are resolved and the user has run `git rebase "
"--continue`, the rebase should open an editor and ask the user to update the "
"commit message.  The 'merge' backend does this, while the 'apply' backend "
"blindly applies the original commit message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:775
#, no-wrap
msgid "Miscellaneous differences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:781
msgid ""
"There are a few more behavioral differences that most folks would probably "
"consider inconsequential but which are mentioned for completeness:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:785
msgid ""
"Reflog: The two backends will use different wording when describing the "
"changes made in the reflog, though both will make use of the word "
"\"rebase\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:791
msgid ""
"Progress, informational, and error messages: The two backends provide "
"slightly different progress and informational messages.  Also, the apply "
"backend writes error messages (such as \"Your files would be "
"overwritten...\") to stdout, while the merge backend writes them to stderr."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:794
msgid ""
"State directories: The two backends keep their state in different "
"directories under `.git/`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:798
#, no-wrap
msgid "NOTES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:803
msgid ""
"You should understand the implications of using `git rebase` on a repository "
"that you share.  See also RECOVERING FROM UPSTREAM REBASE below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:808
msgid ""
"When the rebase is run, it will first execute a `pre-rebase` hook if one "
"exists.  You can use this hook to do sanity checks and reject the rebase if "
"it isn't appropriate.  Please see the template `pre-rebase` hook script for "
"an example."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:810
msgid "Upon completion, `<branch>` will be the current branch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:812
#, no-wrap
msgid "INTERACTIVE MODE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:817
msgid ""
"Rebasing interactively means that you have a chance to edit the commits "
"which are rebased.  You can reorder the commits, and you can remove them "
"(weeding out bad or otherwise unwanted patches)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:819
msgid "The interactive mode is meant for this type of workflow:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:821
msgid "have a wonderful idea"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:822
msgid "hack on the code"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:823
msgid "prepare a series for submission"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:824
msgid "submit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:826
msgid "where point 2. consists of several instances of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:828
msgid "regular use"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:830
msgid "finish something worthy of a commit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:831
msgid "commit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:833
msgid "independent fixup"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:835
msgid "realize that something does not work"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:836
msgid "fix that"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:837
msgid "commit it"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:843
msgid ""
"Sometimes the thing fixed in b.2. cannot be amended to the not-quite perfect "
"commit it fixes, because that commit is buried deeply in a patch series.  "
"That is exactly what interactive rebase is for: use it after plenty of "
"\"a\"s and \"b\"s, by rearranging and editing commits, and squashing "
"multiple commits into one."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:845
msgid "Start it with the last commit you want to retain as-is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:847
#, no-wrap
msgid "\tgit rebase -i <after-this-commit>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:852
msgid ""
"An editor will be fired up with all the commits in your current branch "
"(ignoring merge commits), which come after the given commit.  You can "
"reorder the commits in this list to your heart's content, and you can remove "
"them.  The list looks more or less like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:857
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"pick deadbee The oneline of this commit\n"
"pick fa1afe1 The oneline of the next commit\n"
"...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:862
msgid ""
"The oneline descriptions are purely for your pleasure; 'git rebase' will not "
"look at them but at the commit names (\"deadbee\" and \"fa1afe1\" in this "
"example), so do not delete or edit the names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:867
msgid ""
"By replacing the command \"pick\" with the command \"edit\", you can tell "
"`git rebase` to stop after applying that commit, so that you can edit the "
"files and/or the commit message, amend the commit, and continue rebasing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:870
msgid ""
"To interrupt the rebase (just like an \"edit\" command would do, but without "
"cherry-picking any commit first), use the \"break\" command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:873
msgid ""
"If you just want to edit the commit message for a commit, replace the "
"command \"pick\" with the command \"reword\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:876
msgid ""
"To drop a commit, replace the command \"pick\" with \"drop\", or just delete "
"the matching line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:891
msgid ""
"If you want to fold two or more commits into one, replace the command "
"\"pick\" for the second and subsequent commits with \"squash\" or "
"\"fixup\".  If the commits had different authors, the folded commit will be "
"attributed to the author of the first commit.  The suggested commit message "
"for the folded commit is the concatenation of the first commit's message "
"with those identified by \"squash\" commands, omitting the messages of "
"commits identified by \"fixup\" commands, unless \"fixup -c\" is used.  In "
"that case the suggested commit message is only the message of the \"fixup "
"-c\" commit, and an editor is opened allowing you to edit the message.  The "
"contents (patch) of the \"fixup -c\" commit are still incorporated into the "
"folded commit. If there is more than one \"fixup -c\" commit, the message "
"from the final one is used.  You can also use \"fixup -C\" to get the same "
"behavior as \"fixup -c\" except without opening an editor."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:895
msgid ""
"`git rebase` will stop when \"pick\" has been replaced with \"edit\" or when "
"a command fails due to merge errors. When you are done editing and/or "
"resolving conflicts you can continue with `git rebase --continue`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:899
msgid ""
"For example, if you want to reorder the last 5 commits, such that what was "
"`HEAD~4` becomes the new `HEAD`. To achieve that, you would call `git "
"rebase` like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:902
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git rebase -i HEAD~5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:905
msgid "And move the first patch to the end of the list."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:908
msgid ""
"You might want to recreate merge commits, e.g. if you have a history like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:915
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"           X\n"
"            \\\n"
"         A---M---B\n"
"        /\n"
"---o---O---P---Q\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:919
msgid ""
"Suppose you want to rebase the side branch starting at \"A\" to \"Q\". Make "
"sure that the current `HEAD` is \"B\", and call"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:922
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git rebase -i -r --onto Q O\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:929
msgid ""
"Reordering and editing commits usually creates untested intermediate steps.  "
"You may want to check that your history editing did not break anything by "
"running a test, or at least recompiling at intermediate points in history by "
"using the \"exec\" command (shortcut \"x\").  You may do so by creating a "
"todo list like this one:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:938
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"pick deadbee Implement feature XXX\n"
"fixup f1a5c00 Fix to feature XXX\n"
"exec make\n"
"pick c0ffeee The oneline of the next commit\n"
"edit deadbab The oneline of the commit after\n"
"exec cd subdir; make test\n"
"...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:943
msgid ""
"The interactive rebase will stop when a command fails (i.e. exits with non-0 "
"status) to give you an opportunity to fix the problem. You can continue with "
"`git rebase --continue`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:947
msgid ""
"The \"exec\" command launches the command in a shell (the default one, "
"usually /bin/sh), so you can use shell features (like \"cd\", \">\", \";\" "
"...). The command is run from the root of the working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:950
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git rebase -i --exec \"make test\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:954
msgid ""
"This command lets you check that intermediate commits are compilable.  The "
"todo list becomes like that:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:964
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"pick 5928aea one\n"
"exec make test\n"
"pick 04d0fda two\n"
"exec make test\n"
"pick ba46169 three\n"
"exec make test\n"
"pick f4593f9 four\n"
"exec make test\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:967
#, no-wrap
msgid "SPLITTING COMMITS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:973
msgid ""
"In interactive mode, you can mark commits with the action \"edit\".  "
"However, this does not necessarily mean that `git rebase` expects the result "
"of this edit to be exactly one commit.  Indeed, you can undo the commit, or "
"you can add other commits.  This can be used to split a commit into two:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:977
msgid ""
"Start an interactive rebase with `git rebase -i <commit>^`, where `<commit>` "
"is the commit you want to split.  In fact, any commit range will do, as long "
"as it contains that commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:979
msgid "Mark the commit you want to split with the action \"edit\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:983
msgid ""
"When it comes to editing that commit, execute `git reset HEAD^`.  The effect "
"is that the `HEAD` is rewound by one, and the index follows suit.  However, "
"the working tree stays the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:987
msgid ""
"Now add the changes to the index that you want to have in the first commit.  "
"You can use `git add` (possibly interactively) or `git gui` (or both) to do "
"that."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:990
msgid ""
"Commit the now-current index with whatever commit message is appropriate "
"now."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:992
msgid "Repeat the last two steps until your working tree is clean."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:994
msgid "Continue the rebase with `git rebase --continue`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:999
msgid ""
"If you are not absolutely sure that the intermediate revisions are "
"consistent (they compile, pass the testsuite, etc.) you should use `git "
"stash` to stash away the not-yet-committed changes after each commit, test, "
"and amend the commit if fixes are necessary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1002
#, no-wrap
msgid "RECOVERING FROM UPSTREAM REBASE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1009
msgid ""
"Rebasing (or any other form of rewriting) a branch that others have based "
"work on is a bad idea: anyone downstream of it is forced to manually fix "
"their history.  This section explains how to do the fix from the "
"downstream's point of view.  The real fix, however, would be to avoid "
"rebasing the upstream in the first place."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1014
msgid ""
"To illustrate, suppose you are in a situation where someone develops a "
"'subsystem' branch, and you are working on a 'topic' that is dependent on "
"this 'subsystem'.  You might end up with a history like the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1021
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    o---o---o---o---o---o---o---o  master\n"
"\t \\\n"
"\t  o---o---o---o---o  subsystem\n"
"\t\t\t   \\\n"
"\t\t\t    *---*---*  topic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1024
msgid "If 'subsystem' is rebased against 'master', the following happens:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1031
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    o---o---o---o---o---o---o---o  master\n"
"\t \\\t\t\t \\\n"
"\t  o---o---o---o---o\t  o'--o'--o'--o'--o'  subsystem\n"
"\t\t\t   \\\n"
"\t\t\t    *---*---*  topic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1035
msgid ""
"If you now continue development as usual, and eventually merge 'topic' to "
"'subsystem', the commits from 'subsystem' will remain duplicated forever:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1042
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    o---o---o---o---o---o---o---o  master\n"
"\t \\\t\t\t \\\n"
"\t  o---o---o---o---o\t  o'--o'--o'--o'--o'--M\t subsystem\n"
"\t\t\t   \\\t\t\t     /\n"
"\t\t\t    *---*---*-..........-*--*  topic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1049
msgid ""
"Such duplicates are generally frowned upon because they clutter up history, "
"making it harder to follow.  To clean things up, you need to transplant the "
"commits on 'topic' to the new 'subsystem' tip, i.e., rebase 'topic'.  This "
"becomes a ripple effect: anyone downstream from 'topic' is forced to rebase "
"too, and so on!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1051
msgid "There are two kinds of fixes, discussed in the following subsections:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1052
#, no-wrap
msgid "Easy case: The changes are literally the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1056
msgid ""
"This happens if the 'subsystem' rebase was a simple rebase and had no "
"conflicts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1057
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hard case: The changes are not the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1064
msgid ""
"This happens if the 'subsystem' rebase had conflicts, or used "
"`--interactive` to omit, edit, squash, or fixup commits; or if the upstream "
"used one of `commit --amend`, `reset`, or a full history rewriting command "
"like https://github.com/newren/git-filter-repo[`filter-repo`]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1067
#, no-wrap
msgid "The easy case"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1072
msgid ""
"Only works if the changes (patch IDs based on the diff contents) on "
"'subsystem' are literally the same before and after the rebase 'subsystem' "
"did."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1077
msgid ""
"In that case, the fix is easy because 'git rebase' knows to skip changes "
"that are already present in the new upstream (unless "
"`--reapply-cherry-picks` is given). So if you say (assuming you're on "
"'topic')"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1080
#, no-wrap
msgid "    $ git rebase subsystem\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1082
msgid "you will end up with the fixed history"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1089
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    o---o---o---o---o---o---o---o  master\n"
"\t\t\t\t \\\n"
"\t\t\t\t  o'--o'--o'--o'--o'  subsystem\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t   \\\n"
"\t\t\t\t\t\t    *---*---*  topic\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1093
#, no-wrap
msgid "The hard case"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1097
msgid ""
"Things get more complicated if the 'subsystem' changes do not exactly "
"correspond to the ones before the rebase."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"While an \"easy case recovery\" sometimes appears to be successful\n"
"      even in the hard case, it may have unintended consequences.  For\n"
"      example, a commit that was removed via `git rebase\n"
"      --interactive` will be **resurrected**!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1107
msgid ""
"The idea is to manually tell `git rebase` \"where the old 'subsystem' ended "
"and your 'topic' began\", that is, what the old merge base between them "
"was.  You will have to find a way to name the last commit of the old "
"'subsystem', for example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1111
msgid ""
"With the 'subsystem' reflog: after `git fetch`, the old tip of 'subsystem' "
"is at `subsystem@{1}`.  Subsequent fetches will increase the number.  (See "
"linkgit:git-reflog[1].)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1114
msgid ""
"Relative to the tip of 'topic': knowing that your 'topic' has three commits, "
"the old tip of 'subsystem' must be `topic~3`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1117
msgid ""
"You can then transplant the old `subsystem..topic` to the new tip by saying "
"(for the reflog case, and assuming you are on 'topic' already):"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1120
#, no-wrap
msgid "    $ git rebase --onto subsystem subsystem@{1}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1125
msgid ""
"The ripple effect of a \"hard case\" recovery is especially bad: 'everyone' "
"downstream from 'topic' will now have to perform a \"hard case\" recovery "
"too!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1127
#, no-wrap
msgid "REBASING MERGES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1135
msgid ""
"The interactive rebase command was originally designed to handle individual "
"patch series. As such, it makes sense to exclude merge commits from the todo "
"list, as the developer may have merged the then-current `master` while "
"working on the branch, only to rebase all the commits onto `master` "
"eventually (skipping the merge commits)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1139
msgid ""
"However, there are legitimate reasons why a developer may want to recreate "
"merge commits: to keep the branch structure (or \"commit topology\") when "
"working on multiple, inter-related branches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1144
msgid ""
"In the following example, the developer works on a topic branch that "
"refactors the way buttons are defined, and on another topic branch that uses "
"that refactoring to implement a \"Report a bug\" button. The output of `git "
"log --graph --format=%s -5` may look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*   Merge branch 'report-a-bug'\n"
"|\\\n"
"| * Add the feedback button\n"
"* | Merge branch 'refactor-button'\n"
"|\\ \\\n"
"| |/\n"
"| * Use the Button class for all buttons\n"
"| * Extract a generic Button class from the DownloadButton one\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1161
msgid ""
"The developer might want to rebase those commits to a newer `master` while "
"keeping the branch topology, for example when the first topic branch is "
"expected to be integrated into `master` much earlier than the second one, "
"say, to resolve merge conflicts with changes to the DownloadButton class "
"that made it into `master`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1164
msgid ""
"This rebase can be performed using the `--rebase-merges` option.  It will "
"generate a todo list looking like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1167
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1248
#, no-wrap
msgid "label onto\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1173
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"# Branch: refactor-button\n"
"reset onto\n"
"pick 123456 Extract a generic Button class from the DownloadButton one\n"
"pick 654321 Use the Button class for all buttons\n"
"label refactor-button\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1178
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"# Branch: report-a-bug\n"
"reset refactor-button # Use the Button class for all buttons\n"
"pick abcdef Add the feedback button\n"
"label report-a-bug\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"reset onto\n"
"merge -C a1b2c3 refactor-button # Merge 'refactor-button'\n"
"merge -C 6f5e4d report-a-bug # Merge 'report-a-bug'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1186
msgid ""
"In contrast to a regular interactive rebase, there are `label`, `reset` and "
"`merge` commands in addition to `pick` ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1194
msgid ""
"The `label` command associates a label with the current HEAD when that "
"command is executed. These labels are created as worktree-local refs "
"(`refs/rewritten/<label>`) that will be deleted when the rebase "
"finishes. That way, rebase operations in multiple worktrees linked to the "
"same repository do not interfere with one another. If the `label` command "
"fails, it is rescheduled immediately, with a helpful message how to proceed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1201
msgid ""
"The `reset` command resets the HEAD, index and worktree to the specified "
"revision. It is similar to an `exec git reset --hard <label>`, but refuses "
"to overwrite untracked files. If the `reset` command fails, it is "
"rescheduled immediately, with a helpful message how to edit the todo list "
"(this typically happens when a `reset` command was inserted into the todo "
"list manually and contains a typo)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1207
msgid ""
"The `merge` command will merge the specified revision(s) into whatever is "
"HEAD at that time. With `-C <original-commit>`, the commit message of the "
"specified merge commit will be used. When the `-C` is changed to a "
"lower-case `-c`, the message will be opened in an editor after a successful "
"merge so that the user can edit the message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1210
msgid ""
"If a `merge` command fails for any reason other than merge conflicts (i.e.  "
"when the merge operation did not even start), it is rescheduled immediately."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1221
msgid ""
"By default, the `merge` command will use the `ort` merge strategy for "
"regular merges, and `octopus` for octopus merges.  One can specify a default "
"strategy for all merges using the `--strategy` argument when invoking "
"rebase, or can override specific merges in the interactive list of commands "
"by using an `exec` command to call `git merge` explicitly with a "
"`--strategy` argument.  Note that when calling `git merge` explicitly like "
"this, you can make use of the fact that the labels are worktree-local refs "
"(the ref `refs/rewritten/onto` would correspond to the label `onto`, for "
"example) in order to refer to the branches you want to merge."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1225
msgid ""
"Note: the first command (`label onto`) labels the revision onto which the "
"commits are rebased; The name `onto` is just a convention, as a nod to the "
"`--onto` option."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1232
msgid ""
"It is also possible to introduce completely new merge commits from scratch "
"by adding a command of the form `merge <merge-head>`. This form will "
"generate a tentative commit message and always open an editor to let the "
"user edit it. This can be useful e.g. when a topic branch turns out to "
"address more than a single concern and wants to be split into two or even "
"more topic branches. Consider this todo list:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1239
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"pick 192837 Switch from GNU Makefiles to CMake\n"
"pick 5a6c7e Document the switch to CMake\n"
"pick 918273 Fix detection of OpenSSL in CMake\n"
"pick afbecd http: add support for TLS v1.3\n"
"pick fdbaec Fix detection of cURL in CMake on Windows\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1245
msgid ""
"The one commit in this list that is not related to CMake may very well have "
"been motivated by working on fixing all those bugs introduced by switching "
"to CMake, but it addresses a different concern. To split this branch into "
"two topic branches, the todo list could be edited like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1251
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"pick afbecd http: add support for TLS v1.3\n"
"label tlsv1.3\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1258
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"reset onto\n"
"pick 192837 Switch from GNU Makefiles to CMake\n"
"pick 918273 Fix detection of OpenSSL in CMake\n"
"pick fdbaec Fix detection of cURL in CMake on Windows\n"
"pick 5a6c7e Document the switch to CMake\n"
"label cmake\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-rebase.adoc:1262
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"reset onto\n"
"merge tlsv1.3\n"
"merge cmake\n"
msgstr ""

